==English==
{{was wotd|2006|April|22}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|ML.|en}} {{term|prognosticare|lang=la}}; see {{term|prognostic}} for more.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en-us-prognosticate.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}
* {{IPA|/pɹɒɡˈnɒstɪke͡ɪt/|lang=en}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|prognosticat}}

# {{context|transitive|lang=en}} To [[predict]] or [[forecast]], especially through the application of skill.
#: ''Examining the tea-leaves, she '''prognosticated''' dark days ahead.''
# {{context|transitive|lang=en}} To [[presage]], [[betoken]].
#: ''The bluebells may '''prognosticate''' an early spring this year.''

====Quotations====
{{timeline|
1500s=1598|
1800s=1847|
1900s=1915}}
* '''1598''' — [[w:William Shakespeare|William Shakespeare]], ''[[s:Sonnet 14 (Shakespeare)|Sonnet xiv]]''
*: But from thine eyes my knowledge I derive,<br>And constant stars in them I read such art<br>As 'Truth and beauty shall together thrive,<br>If from thyself, to store thou wouldst convert';<br>Or else of thee this I '''prognosticate''':<br>'Thy end is truth's and beauty's doom and date.'
* {{RQ:Bronte Wuthering|VII}}
*: ...to-morrow I intend lengthening the night till afternoon. I '''prognosticate''' for myself an obstinate cold, at least.
* '''1915''' — [[w:Virginia Woolf|Virginia Woolf]], [http://www.gutenberg.org/files/144/144-h/144-h.htm|''The Voyage Out''] ch. 2
*: All old people and many sick people were drawn, were it only for a foot or two, into the open air, and '''prognosticated''' pleasant things about the course of the world.

====Synonyms====
* [[presage]], [[prophesy]], [[foretell]]

